{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"image","attrs":{"src":"/documentation/white-papers/wp-thumb-29.webp","alt":"Guardrail Design in the AI Agent Era (Part 2: Practice & Implementation)","title":null,"width":"100%","height":null,"caption":""}},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":1},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Guardrail Design in the AI Agent Era: Case Studies, Checklist, and 90-Day Roadmap"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"📖 Estimated reading time: ~15 minutes"}]},{"type":"horizontalRule"},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Key Takeaway (1-Minute Read)"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"In "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"/features/documentation/white-paper/28/ai-agent-guardrails-governance-2026","target":"_blank","rel":"noopener noreferrer nofollow","class":null,"title":null}}],"text":"Part 1"},{"type":"text","text":", we organized AI-agent guardrails into four elements: Permission, Approval, Audit Trail, and Kill Switch."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Part 2 turns knowledge into execution."}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Part 2 Structure"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"What You Get"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"<strong>Chapter 4: 3 Case Studies</strong>"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Concrete understanding of how the four elements work in real cases: PC-operation agents, development AI vulnerabilities, autonomous 5G operations"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"<strong>Chapter 5: Reusable Checklist</strong>"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A one-page diagnostic sheet you can bring to tomorrow’s meeting"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"<strong>Chapter 6: 90-Day Roadmap</strong>"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Practical timeline from PoC to limited rollout to expansion"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"<strong>Appendix: Glossary</strong>"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A shared language so non-technical executives can actively participate"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"MIT Sloan Management Review (2025) found that "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"95% of GenAI pilots fail to prove P&L impact"},{"type":"text","text":". S&P Global also reported "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"42% of AI initiatives were canceled in 2025"},{"type":"text","text":" (up 25 percentage points YoY). The core failure driver is not technology capability, but governance design."}]},{"type":"horizontalRule"},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":1},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Chapter 4. Case Studies: How the Four Elements Work in Reality"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Case 1: Privilege Escalation Risk in PC-Operation Agents — Lessons from Claude Desktop Extensions (DXT)"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"What Happened"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"In February 2026, LayerX disclosed serious design vulnerabilities in Anthropic’s Claude Desktop Extensions (DXT), which enable Claude to directly operate local PC applications."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The core issue: "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"DXT operated with full system privileges without sandboxing"},{"type":"text","text":" (source: "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"https://www.csoonline.com/article/4129820/anthropics-dxt-poses-critical-rce-vulnerability-by-running-with-full-system-privileges.html","target":"_blank","rel":"noopener noreferrer nofollow","class":null,"title":null}}],"text":"CSO Online, 2026"},{"type":"text","text":")."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Risk pattern:"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Low-risk connectors (e.g., calendar reads) and high-risk local execution could be chained autonomously."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Prompt injection via external data (e.g., malicious calendar text) could trigger arbitrary code execution."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"With broad extension usage, blast radius was significant."}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Four-Element Analysis"}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Element"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Gap in This Case"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Required Design"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"<strong>1) Permission</strong>"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Full-system privilege, no scope/ceiling limits"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Action-level least privilege, e.g., calendar read allowed, local write blocked"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"<strong>2) Approval</strong>"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Low-risk to high-risk chain executed with no human approval"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Require approval when risk level escalates across operation chains"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"<strong>3) Audit Trail</strong>"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Opaque extension-call sequence and rationale"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Log full call chain and rationale at each step"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"<strong>4) Kill Switch</strong>"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"No mechanism to detect/stop abnormal chains"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Set depth/blast thresholds; auto-pause and notify on exceedance"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Management Implication"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"For PC-operation agents, governance must include not only what each action can do, but "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"which actions can be combined"},{"type":"text","text":"."}]},{"type":"horizontalRule"},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Case 2: Supply Chain Risk in Development AI — What Claude Code Vulnerabilities Revealed"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"What Happened"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"On February 25, 2026, Check Point Research disclosed multiple serious vulnerabilities in Anthropic’s AI coding tool Claude Code (source: "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"https://research.checkpoint.com/2026/rce-and-api-token-exfiltration-through-claude-code-project-files-cve-2025-59536/","target":"_blank","rel":"noopener noreferrer nofollow","class":null,"title":null}}],"text":"Check Point Research, 2026"},{"type":"text","text":")."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Key point: attacks could trigger simply by cloning and opening a malicious repository."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Notable issues included:"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"CVE-2025-59536"},{"type":"text","text":": command execution via malicious hooks/MCP settings"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"CVE-2026-21852"},{"type":"text","text":": API-token exfiltration by redirecting API traffic through manipulated environment settings"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"GHSA-ph6w"},{"type":"text","text":": hidden shell execution via hooks abuse"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"This highlighted a new AI supply-chain risk: “passive” configuration files can become active execution paths."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Four-Element Analysis"}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Element"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Gap in This Case"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Required Design"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"<strong>1) Permission</strong>"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Config files implicitly allowed execution authority"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Strictly separate config authority from execution authority"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"<strong>2) Approval</strong>"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Outbound communication started before trust confirmation"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Block network activity before user approval by default"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"<strong>3) Audit Trail</strong>"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Difficult to trace what config triggered what command"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Log full chain: config load -> command execution -> destination changes"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"<strong>4) Kill Switch</strong>"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"No auto-block for suspicious API destination switch"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Whitelist destinations, auto-block unknown endpoints, alert admins"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Management Implication"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"AI tool vulnerabilities are not individual developer problems; they are organization-wide supply-chain risks."}]},{"type":"horizontalRule"},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Case 3: Autonomous Critical Infrastructure Operations — Nokia x AWS Agentic AI Network Slicing"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"What Happened"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"In February 2026, Nokia and AWS announced a live proof-of-concept of agentic AI for 5G-Advanced network slicing, with early pilot partners including du (UAE) and Orange (France) (source: "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"https://www.sdxcentral.com/news/nokia-and-aws-let-agentic-ai-take-the-wheel-for-5g-network-slicing/","target":"_blank","rel":"noopener noreferrer nofollow","class":null,"title":null}}],"text":"SDxCentral, 2026"},{"type":"text","text":")."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Unlike traditional AI recommendations, the system autonomously adjusts RAN policies in near real time based on KPI and contextual data."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Why It Matters"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"This is a success-pattern case: gradual autonomy expansion with explicit controls. AWS also stated the solution remained in pilot stage, not production-ready."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Four-Element Application"}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Element"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Nokia x AWS 